# Rust / Cargo build output
/target

# Differential conformance suite — transient run artifacts (never committed).
# Per-cell workspaces, per-area reports, the run summary, and auto-generated
# finding directories are all written beneath the build directory on every run.
# The curated finding set under tests/conformance/findings/ is deliberately
# NOT ignored — it is a committed deliverable.
/target/conformance-work/
/target/conformance-report/
/target/conformance-findings/

# Differential conformance suite — transient artifacts of the golden-record
# regeneration tool, which are the only ones written INSIDE the corpus rather
# than beneath the build directory.
#
# The lock directory is created before a regeneration run and removed by the
# script's exit trap. It survives only a kill -9 or a power loss, and the script
# reclaims a stale one on the next run and says so — but while it is there it
# must not dirty `git status`, and it must never be committable.
tests/conformance/.regen-lock/
# A STALE lock is reclaimed by renaming it into an unpredictably named quarantine
# directory beside it and then discarding both, because a single rename is the only
# operation two contending runs cannot both win. That directory exists for the
# moment between the rename and the removal, and is ignored for the same reason the
# lock is: an interrupted run must not leave something a later `git add` could pick
# up, and it must never dirty `git status` while it is there.
tests/conformance/.regen-lock-stale.*/
# A rewrite is staged beside the record it will replace and installed by a single
# rename, so staging state exists only for the moment between building it and
# installing it. Ignored for the same reason as the lock: an interrupted run must
# not leave something a later `git add` could pick up.
#
# The script stages into an unpredictably named `.regen.XXXXXX` DIRECTORY created
# by `mktemp -d` in the record's own directory — atomic creation is what makes the
# name safe to use — so the directory form is what has to be ignored. The file form
# is listed beside it because a staging file beside the record is the other shape
# this has taken, and ignoring both costs nothing and cannot go stale.
tests/conformance/**/.regen.*/
tests/conformance/**/*.expected.regen.*
